B.U0=new A.d5("What a wake window is and why it runs everything","How long your baby can stay awake before being too tired to fall asleep.",0,36,"A wake window is how long your baby can comfortably stay awake between sleeps without getting overtired.\n\nWhen the window passes, the body releases cortisol, the baby gets wired and it becomes HARDER to fall asleep, not easier. That is why an overtired baby fights sleep.\n\nHow to use it:\n\u2022 Note the time they woke up.\n\u2022 Soninho adds the wake window for their age and shows the ideal time for the next nap.\n\u2022 Start the wind-down 10 to 15 minutes before the ideal time.\n\nSigns the window is closing: staring, rubbing eyes, pulling an ear, yawning, going quiet. If they are already crying and arching, the window has passed. Next time, go earlier.")
B.U8=new A.d5("The 4-month regression: what changed and what to do","Not a regression, a maturation. Sleep becomes adult-like cycles.",3,6,"Around 4 months the brain starts organizing sleep into 45 to 60-minute cycles, like an adult. Between cycles the baby briefly surfaces. If they fell asleep nursing, in arms or being rocked, they will look for the same thing to get back to sleep.\n\nWhat helps:\n\u2022 Put them in the crib drowsy but awake, at least for the first nap of the day.\n\u2022 A truly dark room and constant white noise.\n\u2022 A predictable routine: same sequence every night.\n\u2022 Respect the wake window (about 1h30 to 2h at this age).\n\nIt lasts 2 to 6 weeks. This is not the time to change everything. It is the time to be consistent.")
B.U3=new A.d5("Bedtime routine: the sequence that cues the body","20 to 30 minutes, always the same, always in the same order.",0,36,'The routine does not need to be long. It needs to be the same.\n\nAn example that works for most families:\n1. Warm bath (or just face and hands on no-bath days)\n2. Massage with lotion, dim lights\n3. Pajamas and sleep sack\n4. Feed in the bedroom, low light\n5. A short song or white noise\n6. Crib awake, "good night", leave the room\n\nAfter 2 weeks of repetition the baby starts relaxing just from entering the sequence. Use the Routine tab in Soninho as a checklist.')
B.U4=new A.d5("Baby waking in the night: the 5 most common causes","Hunger, too hot or cold, wrong window, too much day sleep, sleep association.",2,24,"1. Real hunger: night feeds are normal up to 6 months. After that, talk to your pediatrician before dropping them.\n\n2. Temperature: overheating is the most common issue. Light clothing, indirect fan. Cold hands and feet are normal; check the back of the neck.\n\n3. Bedtime too late or too early: a bedtime outside the window causes 4 or 5 a.m. wakings.\n\n4. Too much day sleep: add up the naps in History. If it is above the typical total for the age, the night gets shorter.\n\n5. Association: falls asleep in arms and wakes in the crib not knowing where they are. Work on the first nap of the day in the crib awake.\n\nLog night wakings in Soninho for a week. The pattern shows up.")
B.TZ=new A.d5("Is white noise harmful? How to use it safely","It helps a lot, as long as it is quiet and away from the crib.",0,36,"White noise mimics the womb and masks household sounds. It works especially well up to 6 months.\n\nRules:\n\u2022 Low volume: you should be able to talk over it. Up to 50 dB, about a shower heard from another room.\n\u2022 Speaker or phone at least 6 feet from the crib.\n\u2022 Fine to leave on all night, continuously. Switching it on and off is what disrupts.\n\u2022 Constant sound (white, pink, rain), not music with variation.\n\nIn Soninho, the Sounds tab loops with a timer or all night.")
B.TW=new A.d5("Sleep at daycare and grandma's: how to align","You cannot control everything. You can protect the night.",4,36,"At daycare babies usually sleep less and worse. That is normal. What you control:\n\n\u2022 Send the routine in writing: wake window, typical nap times, what soothes them. Daycare will thank you.\n\u2022 Share Soninho with the caregiver (Plus) so they log naps. You see it in real time and adjust the night.\n\u2022 If day sleep was short, move bedtime 30 to 45 minutes earlier. Do not try to make it up with a late nap.\n\u2022 At grandma's, bring the sleep sack and the white noise. Familiar smell and sound matter more than the crib.")
B.TT=new A.d5("From 2 naps to 1: when and how","Usually between 13 and 18 months. Do not force it early.",11,20,"Signs it is time:\n\u2022 Refuses the morning nap for more than 2 weeks in a row.\n\u2022 Or the morning nap gets long and the afternoon nap disappears.\n\u2022 Handles 5 or more hours awake in a good mood.\n\nHow to do it:\n\u2022 Push the morning nap 15 minutes later every 2 or 3 days until it lands around noon.\n\u2022 On hard days, move bedtime earlier. A 6:30 p.m. bedtime is normal during the transition.\n\u2022 It takes 2 to 4 weeks. Two-nap days along the way are normal.")
